# Commissioning Guide — Trident 300 → MadMax Two-Tool

Scope: mechanical build is done. This covers wiring sanity through
first T0 print, then the MadMax conversion, T1 bring-up, toolchanger
tuning, and the first dual-tool print. Companion files: INSTALL.md
(software/flashing), README.md (config architecture), config/
(the skeleton configs).

Strategy note, worth internalizing before starting: the printer is
built with the MadMax core (carriage + plate + clips) and T0 from day
one — no interim stock carriage. But commission it as a
SINGLE-TOOL printer first, completely, including a real print: no
docks installed, no toolchanger software loaded, T1 still in its box
until Phase 4. Every toolchanger problem you hit later is then a
toolchanger problem — not a mystery that might be wiring, might be
config, might be docks. Bonus: the one novel mechanical element, the
Maxwell coupling, gets proven in isolation during boring single-tool
printing.

---

## Phase 0 — Pre-power checks (multimeter, PSU off)

MadMax core is part of the initial build: print the core parts
(carriage, plate, clips) and T0's toolhead plate in ABS/ASA and
install them during gantry assembly. Do NOT print/install docks, the
T1 plate, or the Nudge mount yet — those wait for Phase 4. While
assembling, confirm the MadMax carriage geometry puts the X endstop
trigger somewhere sane (or adjust the switch mount now — far easier
during assembly than after).

1. Continuity-check heater circuits: bed SSR wiring, both hotend
   heater pairs. No continuity from any heater lead to frame ground.
2. Verify 24V and mains separation at the Leviathan terminals;
   confirm PSU output voltage unloaded before connecting the board.
3. Check thermistor resistance at room temp (~100kΩ for the usual
   NTC 100K/104NT parts) on bed + both toolheads.
4. Tug-test every crimp. The toolhead umbilicals especially — they
   will move millions of cycles.
5. Wire the Maxwell-coupling detect circuit for T0 now, even though
   toolchanger software comes much later (Phase 5).
6. Set Z endstop-related hardware aside mentally: there is none.
   Cartographer is the Z reference; nothing to wire at the bed rear.

## Phase 1 — Software + electronics bring-up (T0 only mounted)

1. Follow INSTALL.md through flashing the Leviathan, ONE Nitehawk
   (labeled T0), and installing the Cartographer plugin. Leave the
   second Nitehawk in its box.
2. Copy the config package in; fill in the three serial paths
   (leviathan, nhk0, scanner). COMMENT OUT for now:
   `[include toolhead-T1.cfg]`, `[include toolchanger.cfg]`,
   `[include toolchanger_macros.cfg]`, `[include
   homing_toolchanger.cfg]`, `[include nudge.cfg]`, plus the
   toolchanger block at the bottom of toolhead-T0.cfg
   ([gcode_macro T0] / [tool T0] / [tool_probe T0]). Temporarily
   rename `[fan_generic part_fan_t0]` to plain `[fan]` so stock
   M106 drives it. Result: a normal single-tool printer. All of
   this reverts at Phase 5.
3. Klipper starts with no errors; all three MCUs connect.
4. `QUERY_ENDSTOPS` while hand-pressing X, then Y switches:
   open → TRIGGERED transitions on the right axis.
5. Thermistors: bed and extruder read plausible room temp.
6. `SET_HEATER_TEMPERATURE HEATER=heater_bed TARGET=50` — watch it
   climb, then off. Same brief test for the extruder at 100°C.
   Confirm the hotend fan spins at 60°C and reports RPM in Mainsail.
7. `STEPPER_BUZZ STEPPER=stepper_x` (etc. for y, z, z1, z2) —
   confirm each motor moves and direction conventions look right.
   Wrong direction: invert the dir_pin with `!`, don't rewire.
8. Coupling detect check: with toolchanger software not yet loaded,
   watch the detect pin raw — add a temporary
   `[gcode_button t0_detect]` on the detect pin with a RESPOND in
   press/release gcode, or QUERY the pin however convenient. Seat
   and unseat T0 by hand (motors off): transitions must be clean and
   instant, every time. This is the Maxwell coupling's health check,
   done in isolation.

## Phase 2 — Motion, probing, and calibration (still single-tool)

1. Home X, then Y. Verify homing direction and that position_endstop
   matches physical reality (nozzle at endstop = the coordinate in
   config). Fix position_max to measured travel.
2. Cartographer: with XY homed, `CARTOGRAPHER_CALIBRATE` at bed
   center per its docs (manual paper-Z first run), then `G28 Z`.
   Run `PROBE_ACCURACY`; expect single-digit-micron range.
3. `Z_TILT_ADJUST` — should converge in 2-3 rounds. Re-`G28 Z`.
4. `BED_MESH_CALIBRATE` — sanity-check the mesh shape (no cliffs).
5. PID tune: `PID_CALIBRATE HEATER=heater_bed TARGET=100`,
   `PID_CALIBRATE HEATER=extruder TARGET=245`, SAVE_CONFIG.
6. Extruder rotation_distance calibration: mark 120mm, extrude 100,
   measure, correct.
7. Input shaper with the T0 ADXL: `SHAPER_CALIBRATE`. Expect to
   revisit after MadMax (mass changes).
8. First layer: `CARTOGRAPHER_TOUCH` z-offset workflow, test square,
   dial in.

## Phase 3 — First T0 print

A real print: a Voron cube or similar, PLA or ABS as you prefer.
You are proving: homing, meshing, extrusion, cooling, retraction,
PRINT_START/PRINT_END macros — and the Maxwell coupling under real
print loads. Run at least one print at your intended full
accelerations and inspect layer consistency; coupling micro-movement
shows up as artifacts here. Slice with a plain single-extruder
profile at this stage. Do not proceed until this print is boring.

Also record a coupling baseline now: a PROBE_ACCURACY-style
repeatability number and a photo of a test print. The MadMax docs
note the lower coupling screws wear over time — a baseline gives you
something objective to compare against months from now.

Milestone: the machine is a working Trident. Take the config backup
now (`~/printer_data/config` → git or a dated tarball). This is the
known-good state you can diff against for everything that follows.

## Phase 4 — MadMax conversion, part 2: docks + T1 hardware

The core is already on the printer; this phase adds everything else.

1. Print TWO docks and the T1 toolhead plate in ABS/ASA, plus the
   Nudge mount.
2. Install docks per the repo's INSTRUCTIONS.md; route the second
   umbilical; mount the Nudge at its chosen rear-bed location (both
   nozzles must reach it — dry-fit and check against dock keep-out
   before committing).
3. Wire the Nudge to its main-board endstop input, and the T1
   coupling detect circuit.
4. Re-measure anything dock hardware changed (Y travel near docks);
   expect to re-run input shaper if head hardware changed mass.
5. Flash the second Nitehawk (INSTALL.md §3), label everything T1.

## Phase 5 — Config cutover to toolchanger

1. Restore the toolchanger config: uncomment the T1, toolchanger,
   toolchanger_macros, homing_toolchanger, and nudge includes plus
   toolhead-T0.cfg's toolchanger block; revert `[fan]` back to
   `[fan_generic part_fan_t0]`; comment out the simple
   [homing_override] in macros.cfg (homing_toolchanger.cfg
   supersedes it); set safe_home_x/y in [gcode_macro
   homing_variables] to bed center for Cartographer touch. Klipper
   restarts clean. The dock positions are still -1000 sentinels --
   any toolchange attempt now fails loudly instead of moving, which
   is intended until Phase 6 replaces them with measurements.
3. Verify tool detection before any motion: with T0 seated,
   the toolchanger status shows tool 0 detected; pop T0 off by hand
   (printer idle, motors off) and watch detection drop. Same for T1
   seated by hand.
4. Re-verify Phase 1 checks for the T1 head: extruder1 thermistor,
   heater blip, hotend fan + tach, extruder direction, PID tune.

## Phase 6 — Dock calibration (the careful part)

Work with motors at reduced current if nervous, hand on the killswitch,
and speeds low. Never do this with heaters on.

1. With T0 mounted, jog the carriage until the tool is perfectly
   seated in its dock keyslot position. `GET_POSITION`. That's
   params_park_x/y for T0. Repeat for T1's dock (mount T1 by hand
   for this).
2. Walk through a dropoff manually: issue the individual moves of
   the dock path at 300 mm/min and watch clearances. Adjust path
   params. Then a pickup. Then let the macro do it at
   params_path_speed ~600.
3. `T0`, `T1`, `T0` ... individually, watching every change. When
   confident, `DOCK_SEQUENCE REPS=10`, then REPS=100 at full
   params_fast_speed. Zero detection faults is the bar — a 99%
   toolchanger ruins every long print.
4. Confirm crash detection: with a tool attached and the printer
   idle-but-homed, gently pull the tool off by hand — the configured
   crash_gcode should fire immediately.

## Phase 7 — Tool offsets

1. Locate the Nudge: `G28`, `NUDGE_MOVE_OVER_PROBE` coordinates set
   from jogging T0's nozzle ~1mm above the pin, then
   `NUDGE_FIND_TOOL_OFFSET`. Verify sane "Sensor location at ..."
   console output and repeatability (run twice; compare).
2. Install r2pdx's two .cfg files, extend the variables block for
   two tools, and run `TC_FIND_ALL_TOOL_OFFSETS`. Offsets land in
   the save file; T0 stays 0,0,0 by definition.
3. Verify Z agreement: after offsets, print a one-layer patch with
   T0 and another with T1 — first-layer squish should match. Fine
   tune per r2pdx's live-adjust workflow (0.05mm X/Y, 0.01-0.025mm
   Z increments).
4. Print an alignment test (two-color calibration cross / offset
   test STL) and inspect seams under magnification. You have a
   camera and macro rig; use it — layer-line-level misalignment is
   easier to judge in a photo.

## Phase 8 — First dual-tool print

1. Slicer: multi-extruder profile (2 extruders, same nozzle size),
   tool-change gcode = `T[next_extruder]`, PRINT_START line passing
   BED, T0_TEMP, T1_TEMP (see klipper-toolchanger's example slicer
   snippets for the is_extruder_used conditionals).
2. Add `VERIFY_TOOL_DETECTED ASYNC=1` to after-layer-change gcode —
   cheap insurance that a silently-dropped tool stops the print at
   the next layer.
3. First print: a two-color Voron cube or similar small two-material
   part. Watch the first three toolchanges in person.
4. Ooze/prime tuning comes last: dock brushes/blockers, prime tower
   vs purge line, per-tool pressure advance. Expect a few evenings
   of iteration here; it's normal.

---

## Sequencing rationale / common traps

- MadMax core from day one, but single-tool software first (Phases
  1-3) — final geometry/mass from the start, coupling proven in
  isolation, and every later fault is unambiguously a toolchanger
  fault.
- Backup at Phase 3 milestone; diff configs afterward, don't rewrite.
- Detection verification (5.3) before motion means a path bug can't
  masquerade as a detection bug later.
- Offsets (Phase 7) only after docks are 100% reliable (Phase 6) —
  calibrating offsets against inconsistent seating chases ghosts.
- Re-run input shaper after ANY toolhead mass change, including
  adding dock hardware to heads.
- Keep klipper-toolchanger pinned to the commit you commissioned on;
  update deliberately with the changelog open.
